{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1126703-storyteller-new-to-wod-and-m-ta-looking-for-input-and-advice-on-campaign-hooks","id":1126703,"title":"Storyteller new to WoD and M:TA looking for input and advice on campaign hooks","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-08-09T07:37:50-07:00","id":1126703,"title":"Storyteller new to WoD and M:TA looking for input and advice on campaign hooks","content":"Hello everyone <br /> <br /> As per topic, I want to ask for some input. I have experience with running Pathfinder and Star Wars but am completely new to WoD. When I first heard about Mage I immediately fell in love with the setting, and the more I learn about it the more I want to run it. <br /> <br /> So.. I'm almost done with reading through the M20 rulebook and it's time to start thinking about the chronicle. The players will most likely be new to Mage, so I don't really want to<br /> plunge them (nor myself) into the middle of the ascension war right off the bat...<br /> <br /> I've got these two ideas floating around:<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>The characters are a gaming group pf childhood friends (like in Stranger Things, but adult-ish). They are attending a convention and are cosplaying as their favorite heroes. During the event something extreme happens (perhaps an attack of some &quot;League of Evil&quot;, very indirectly instigated by a Nephandi) that triggers their awakenings. Their focus', avatars and powers would reflect who they were cosplaying. I imagine they would then form some sort of vigilante Justice League and fight crime.</li> <li>The characters are literal orphans that have all been raised in a very special orphanage. Something between <i>Foster's Home for Imaginary Friends </i>and Xavier's School for Gifted Youngsters, being run by a mage (ex-tradition, now a chaos mage, who used the events of the avatar storm to disappear?) who also is a mentor for the characters all the other kids there. The mentor uses his powers coupled with a network of contacts among Child Protective Services to identify and transfer pre-awakened or recently-awakened children from the streets, other orphanages or foster families in no way prepared to raise a young mage. The chronicle could be set at various stages:<ul><li>during or shortly after transferring to the institution</li> <li>pre-adult, still living there and learning how to control and use their powers</li> <li>adults, out in the big world, helping out with some problems the institution has</li> </ul></li> </ol>I think that the first hook is simpler and presents more intuitive character concepts and paradigms for the players, it would most likely stay on the street level for a good while.<br /> The second one probably has more potential for a longer campaign but is also more complicated on many levels. I expect that striking the balance between making the place insignificant enough not to attract too much attention from the big factions and significant enough to offer interesting hooks will be one of the major challenges. <br /> <br /> <br /> Are the hooks any good, especially for a introduction to the system and setting? Any suggestions, weak points, things that I'm missing, etc?<br /> Or should I try something else (or use the hooks from the &quot;1000 Mage Plots&quot; thread)?<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13711-kranf-niest","name":"Kranf Niest","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-09T08:36:06-07:00","id":1126726,"title":"","content":"Rather than force your players into any one particular type of character and story, I would suggest you embrace the diversity of Mage by allowing them to create characters they find interesting and craft a story based on what they present to you. In my experience, limited to the last two years as it is, the best way to get someone invested in Mage is to cater to their desires. Find out from the players which parts of the game sound most appealing and run with that. Edit: they may not be interested in vigilante crime fighting at all.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12710-pazuzuaxelf","name":"PazuzuAxelf","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-09T08:43:02-07:00","id":1126731,"title":"","content":"*The second one has an element that can be great for starting players: A mentor-like figure avalible for free (some purist would demand Mentor Backround, but I would give it for free in this case because it's part of the story), that's has a place of teaching. This allows you to show them how do you want to use magic, and to evacuate doubts IOC.<br /> <br /> *What it a player does not want to be an orphan/cosplayer? , of course you can enforce these elements, but one needs to think about it. I<br /> <br /> I think both are OK concepts/hocks, though I like the 2nd more, but I agree with PazuzuAxelf on this.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 08-09-2017, 11:46 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-09T10:57:45-07:00","id":1126774,"title":"","content":"One note on the vigilante crime fighting: how subtle are they about it, and do you plan on maintaining the setting at all? I definitely understand not plunging into the Ascension War right away (unless a character or most of your characters really want to go there). It's often best to start off small, especially with a new group. They can roleplay learning how to employ certain effects, developing their own rotes, etc. etc. Both the players and the characters can learn together, so to speak. A mentor can chime in and educate them on the history of the Ascension War and what they need to do to remain safe from the Technocracy when you think it's appropriate to 'have the talk' with the characters. Also, the Avatar Storm is not something I would say you need to have in your Mage setting (either it didn't happen at all, or it was temporary and subsided). The Avatar Storm really shits hard on any character who wants to be a Spirit Mage, and part of the reason it occurred in the older editions was to lead up to the end-of-times scenario for the game. Unless you are looking to herald the apocalypse/last shot at Ascension, there is no need to have the Avatar Storm be a thing (or at least for it to be a permanent thing). It could help to have it occur to have cut off/killed the elder leadership of the Traditions and Technocracy if you want to avoid having the characters get preoccupied with cavorting about in mystical realms in the Umbra (e.g., Doissetep or Horizon), but there seems to me to be little need to have the storm persist.<br /> <br /> The one issue if they are vigilante crime fighters, you will need to come up with a reason why, as the ST, the Technocracy does not clamp down on them hard. Do the players do anything that appears to be supernatural? If they do, why would the Technocracy leave them alone (they likely wouldn't). If you had vigilante crime fighters who operated more like Batman, the Technocracy might let them do their thing for a while. But if your players are shooting fireballs and flying (the latter might not even be possible in light of Sleeper disbelief), then the Technocracy is going to slam down on it hard (in the default setting).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12988-imperator","name":"Imperator","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12988_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"41"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-09T16:46:26-07:00","id":1126885,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PazuzuAxelf</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1126703-storyteller-new-to-wod-and-m-ta-looking-for-input-and-advice-on-campaign-hooks?p=1126726#post1126726\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Rather than force your players into any one particular type of character and story, I would suggest you embrace the diversity of Mage by allowing them to create characters they find interesting and craft a story based on what they present to you. In my experience, limited to the last two years as it is, the best way to get someone invested in Mage is to cater to their desires. Find out from the players which parts of the game sound most appealing and run with that. Edit: they may not be interested in vigilante crime fighting at all.</div> </div> </div> </div>My reasoning behind such a narrow (at least for Mage standards) character and story concept is to provide very stron binding ties for the party. Both the rulebook and stuff I've read online emphasize that having a group from different traditions/crafts can be problematic, because they are expected to have totally different paradigms and &quot;forced&quot; to believe that only their paradigm is right. Which, if everyone is roleplaying their characters properly, leads directly to a lot of conflict or at best everyone wanting to do their own thing rather than doing things together.<br /> <br /> On a more general note, I don't intend to railroad my players into a chronicle they won't enjoy. We'll certainly have a session zero, where I'll introduce everyone to the setting, talk about what they'd want to do and pitch them the hooks I mentioned above and possibly some others as well. If they like one of the hooks, great. If not, I'll come up with something more in line with their expectations. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">*The second one has an element that can be great for starting players: A mentor-like figure available for free (some purist would demand Mentor Background, but I would give it for free in this case because it's part of the story), that's has a place of teaching. This allows you to show them how do you want to use magic, and to evacuate doubts IOC.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, I intend to give it as a free pooled background, possibly both mentor and chantry. And it could turn out that the initially 1 or 2 dot mentor didn't reveal the true extend of his powers, and could sort of grow along with the characters. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Imperator</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">One note on the vigilante crime fighting: how subtle are they about it, and do you plan on maintaining the setting at all?</div> </div> </div> </div>I intend to maintain the setting, maybe with some slight alterations but nothing major. And the subtlety will be up to the players. They'll certainly be briefed on the Technocracy, at least OOCly.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Imperator</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Also, the Avatar Storm is not something I would say you need to have in your Mage setting (either it didn't happen at all, or it was temporary and subsided).</div> </div> </div> </div>I want to go for the meta-plot variant recommended by M20, where the Avatar Storm was shattering but not permanent. This gives the mentor the opportunity to both fall of the radar and found the institution without attracting too much attention, as the Traditions and Technocracy would have more important matters to attend to.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Imperator</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"> The one issue if they are vigilante crime fighters, you will need to come up with a reason why, as the ST, the Technocracy does not clamp down on them hard.</div> </div> </div> </div>If they decide to go the vigilante route (it seems like an obvious choice to me, which probably means the players will do the exact opposite...) I hope they won't go overboard, as that would indeed be asking for a &quot;friendly&quot; visit from the Men in Black.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13711-kranf-niest","name":"Kranf Niest","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-09T17:33:29-07:00","id":1126899,"title":"","content":"The only advice I could offer that hasn't been already stated is talking to your players first. Find out what kind of story each of them want, and what kind of character they would like to play. Even if you want them to have roughly the same origins, find out what they would like to be in that framework and what kind of character they wish to build. Craft your story with these elements in mind, and never plan too far ahead.<br /> <br /> Honestly, having your players be all from different traditions is not nearly as difficult to work with as the book makes it out to be. It actually makes things a lot more interesting when they have to figure out how to work with one another in order to get things done.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/959615-chronicle-report-keepers-of-the-wyck\" target=\"_blank\">Keepers of the Wyck: A Chronicle I'm running</a> FINALE: Chapter 39: Green Fairy","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12193-saikou","name":"Saikou","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12193_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-10T06:48:20-07:00","id":1127092,"title":"","content":"The organization of the Traditions it's one that the basic does not emphasize enough.<br /> <br /> The Traditions aren't a bunch of divided mages thinking they're right and everyone else's it's wrong, they are an organization dedicated to the preservation of magic, they offer mentorship services and cross-tradition resources trough a system of boons. They have (or at least had) an agenda that justifies forming cross-tradition chantries and other social structures that transcend individual Traditions.<br /> The average Traditionalist doesn't have a closed Paradigm where other beliefs are just wrong, most of them recognize that other Paradigms work, but preffer theirs (Because of the influence of cross-tradition cabals, chantries, and factions that have existed within the Traditions from day 1, I imagine a lone Orphan or Disparate it's more likely to think their Paradigm it's right and everyone else's it's wrong just because it's less exposed to the pov of others).<br /> <br /> Belonging to the Traditions/Technocracy has a few advantages from the pov of the players too:<br /> *You don't have to think so much on your Paradigm. Sure, you need to research to play a Hermetic with a complex Partadigm direct from the tabula smaragdina, the briefing of the Traditions suggest a harry potter-but that's already a Paradigm, that's already a framework to understand what your magic can/would do.<br /> <br /> *You have something to do: Each traditions has goals, and the Traditions may have plans for the city (for instance, a chantry or a cabal could be founded in the city with the purpose of recruitting promising orphans) . Technocracy will always have jobs for you.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 08-10-2017, 09:52 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-10T07:28:04-07:00","id":1127101,"title":"","content":"That kind of approach makes a cross-tradition party much easier to pull off.<br /> <br /> But it doesn't exactly fit in with how it's described in M20. For example, the stereotypical approach to fellow traditions by the Verbena is:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> We\u2019re siblings of the blood \u2013 quarreling, dysfunctional, often hating one another\u2019s guts\u2026 but we\u2019ll<br /> kick anyone else\u2019s ass if they mess with us. One for all, for better and worse. </div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13711-kranf-niest","name":"Kranf Niest","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-10T09:14:47-07:00","id":1127159,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kranf Niest</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1126703-storyteller-new-to-wod-and-m-ta-looking-for-input-and-advice-on-campaign-hooks?p=1127101#post1127101\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That kind of approach makes a cross-tradition party much easier to pull off.<br /> <br /> But it doesn't exactly fit in with how it's described in M20.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh, I forgot you're new to WoD. In WoD everyone hates each other. It has little to do with Traditions and more to do with the setting itself. <br /> <br /> This it's because WoD often plays with the idea of players having antagonic interests, in a Chantry you can have different groups of mages trying to take control (politicaly, rarely by force) to push their personal agendas, but you also have group agendas that justify comming together. <br /> <br /> For instance: A VA hacker may live in a supertechnological building, while a Verbena would be more comfortable close to the nature. They have to protect an Urban Node. Now, they may have a lot to argue about where they want to spend the Quint (Better equipment v.s. paying a God), but they will both defend the place .","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-10T18:48:12-07:00","id":1127434,"title":"","content":"Yes, in WoD, everyone's group is always superior to their allies because their group is right per the quotes. The Traditions as a group and the Technocracy as a group may have disagreements amongst members with specific paradigms, but there are some areas of general agreement. A Verbena looks at the natural world and places that in higher importance than the Virtual Adept, but they'll both be working for Ascension away from the Technocracy's attempt to control science.<br /> <br /> Most of the sphere writeups have in-game flavor, but the codification of the spheres makes it so Tradition mages who agree on enough can cast spells together and not ruin each other's paradigms. Since there is even variation within each Tradition, unless there are some serious paradigm disagreements, you can assume members of the Traditions can work together. Now there may be conflicts over tactics, goals, strategy, philosophy and other subjects, but a Verbena and a Virtual Adept could be very good friends. If said Verbena was a technopagan, they'd probably get along real well with a Virtual Adept.<br /> <br /> Since Mage is a game about playing someone who changes reality with their beliefs, let your players choose character types they'd enjoy playing. The Justice League isn't a bad idea, but I think having the PCs at the same school provides more structure. A third option is that all the PCs get called together by their mentors or chantries that they've just graduated from to perform a mission.<br /> <br /> I'll be going with the third option, adjusted for what the players do with their characters, for my next game. They'll all be in Portland, Oregon, for a reason and they'll all meet early on.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13680-baakyocalder","name":"baakyocalder","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13680_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"353"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}